{
  "dom_id": "step-q31478",
  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "Calculate the integral $\\int \\frac{{5}^{x}}{{5}^{x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
  "worked_solution": "Notice that the numerator is proportional to the derivative of the denominator. So, we introduce the new variable $u={5}^{x}+1.$ Differentiating gives \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}={5}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}\u20615\\,\u27f9\\,\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}\u20615}\\,\\text{d}u={5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{5}^{x}}{{5}^{x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{5}^{x}+1}\\cdot {5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}\u20615}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}\u20615}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}\u20615}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}\u2061|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}\u20615}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}\u2061|{5}^{x}+1|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({5}^{x}+1)}{\\operatorname{ln}\u20615}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, in the last step, we used the fact that ${5}^{x}+1>0$ for all $x.$",
